What an intriguing idea! Creating a JavaScript visualization of "The World in Two Weeks" by Hieronymus Bosch, depicting it as a Zip Bomb.

For those unfamiliar, Hieronymus Bosch's triptych "The Garden of Earthly Delights" is a surreal and enigmatic masterpiece, featuring vivid depictions of fantastical creatures and abstract scenes. "Die Welt in Zwo Wochen" (The World in Two Weeks) is a smaller, intricate painting that might be interpreted as a representation of the world's chaos and destruction.

A Zip Bomb, on the other hand, is a programming concept where a ZIP file contains a self-replicating code that, when extracted, expands to consume all available system resources and crashes the system. In the context of your request, we'll use this concept to create a mesmerizing visual representation of Bosch's work.

Here's a basic implementation in JavaScript using the HTML5 canvas element:
```javascript
// Set up the canvas
const canvas = document.getElementById('canvas');
const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');

// Set the dimensions of the canvas
canvas.width = 800;
canvas.height = 600;

// Define the image data URL of the Zip Bomb
const zipBombURL = 'data:image/jpeg;base64,BASE64_ENCODED_IMAGE_DATA';

// Load the image data
fetch(zipBombURL)
  .then(response => response.arrayBuffer())
  .then(arrayBuffer => {
    // Convert the array buffer to a Blobs
    const blob = new Blob([arrayBuffer], { type: 'image/jpeg' });
    // Create an image object from the blob
    const image = new Image();
    image.src = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
    image.onload = () => {
      // Draw the image on the canvas
      ctx.drawImage(image, 0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);
    };
  });

// Animation loop to create a "Zip Bomb" effect
function animate() {
  // Loop through the canvas pixels and extract the image data
  for (let x = 0; x < canvas.width; x++) {
    for (let y = 0; y < canvas.height; y++) {
      // Get the pixel data
      const pixel = ctx.getImageData(x, y, 1, 1).data;

      // Calculate the noise value
      const noiseValue = Math.random() * 255;

      // Apply the noise value to the pixel
      pixel[0] = pixel[0] + noiseValue;
      pixel[1] = pixel[1] + noiseValue;
      pixel[2] = pixel[2] + noiseValue;

      // Set the pixel data back to the canvas
      ctx.putImageData(ctx.createImageData(1, 1, pixel), x, y);
    }
  }

  // Request the next frame
  requestAnimationFrame(animate);
}

